  "worked_solution": "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =9y+6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(9y+6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =9(1)+6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
